# {{ name }}

{{ description }}

A WhatsApp bot as a plain kdeps workflow: Meta's webhook calls `/webhook`, the
`llm` resource answers the message, `reply` sends the answer with the WhatsApp
Cloud API, and `respond` acknowledges the webhook.

```text
WhatsApp -> POST /webhook -> llm -> reply (Graph API /messages) -> 200 ok
Meta     -> GET  /webhook -> respond (hub.challenge when the verify token matches)
```

## Quick Start

1. Set the credentials from your Meta app (WhatsApp > API Setup):
   ```bash
   export WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=1234567890     # the sending number's ID
   export WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=EAAG...           # system user or temporary token
   export WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN=pick-any-string   # also entered in Meta's webhook form
   export WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=abc123...           # App settings > Basic; signs each POST
   export KDEPS_API_AUTH_TOKEN=dev-token          # /webhook is public; other routes need it
   ```

2. Run it and expose port {{ port }} over HTTPS (Meta only calls HTTPS URLs):
   ```bash
   kdeps run workflow.yaml
   # e.g. cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:{{ port }}
   ```

3. In Meta's webhook settings, set the callback URL to `https://<host>/webhook`
   and the verify token to `WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN`, then subscribe to `messages`.

## How it works

- `GET /webhook` returns `hub.challenge` as plain text only when
  `hub.verify_token` equals `WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN` (403 otherwise).
- `POST /webhook` is checked against `X-Hub-Signature-256`
  (`hmacSHA256(WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET, request.rawBody)`); a forged POST gets 403
  before any model call. Leave `WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET` unset only for local tests.
- Status callbacks (sent, delivered, read) have no text message: `llm` and
  `reply` are skipped and the webhook gets `ok`.
- The first text message of each notification is answered.

Change the model or persona in `resources/llm.yaml`. `WHATSAPP_API_URL`
overrides the Graph API base (default `https://graph.facebook.com/v19.0`).
